            </strong>
            and continuing thereafter on the <strong>
                <apex:outputText value="{0,date,d}"><!--<apex:param value="{!StartDate}"/>--></apex:outputText>
            </strong> of each month until Attorney’s services are completed or terminated.Client agrees to have these amounts automatically withdrawn or debited each month by filing out the attached Credit Card or ACH Pre-Authorization Form. 
            <strong><u> If the payment is not made in the month that it is due, Attorney will immediately start taking 
            steps to be relieved as counsel in any pending action.</u></strong>   
            The cost of services will go up 5% on each one year anniversary of retention.  
            The client understands that most of these cases resolve before trial.  Therefore, if this case 
            proceeds to within one month of trial, the flat fee payment arrangement will terminate and a new agreement will be entered into between Attorney and Client to cover trial expenses and attorneys fees.
        </p>            
    </div>
   <div style="margin-left:20px;">
    <div style="margin-top:-5px; padding:10px 15px; background-color:#FFFFFF; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0;">
        <p style="margin:0 0 10px 0; text-align:justify;">
            a. IMPORTANCE OF MONTHLY PAYMENTS:
            Client acknowledges that the $2,000.00 monthly payment is due every month and that failure to pay the Firm this amount is grounds for Firm’s immediate withdrawal from any lawsuit filed. 
            If client does not pay his/her monthly flat rate payment on the date that it is due, client will be liable for the full hourly amount that Firm has spent on Client’s case, less the monthly 
            flat rate payments that were received. Client understands that litigation at an hourly rate is expensive – for instance – drafting the initial complaint might run as much as $10,000.00 itself, 
            if a client paid for that hourly, under the rates set forth in this agreement. However, client will not be responsible for the hourly rates if the client pays the monthly retainer.
        </p>          
    </div>
</div>

  <div style="margin-left:20px;">
